(⌐■_■) **No candidate meeting either requested frontier.** I obtained a small new improvement and a broader exclusion for single-redex compression.

The `c` let can be placed immediately before the `f` binder:

```text
\b. p ((\C f.
  b (C (\e a. f (\x y. x a (y e))))
    (p (\g. C (\e. f e (g e)))))
  c)
```

**Verified:** `q=99, F=3, q+F=102`; both laws pass. This differs from your `q=100` outer `c` let and saves one bit, but remains outside the valuable range.

**Family exclusion:** Every source that contracts to literal `Q0` in **one β-step**, at any location, satisfies:

| Source free-leaf count | Minimum q |
|---|---:|
| F=4 | 98 |
| F=3 | 99 |
| F=2 | Impossible |

This covers arbitrary capture-safe argument extraction, including sharing only a subset of matching occurrences—not just top-level lets.

The size argument is short. For an inverse contraction replacing selected occurrences of a variable of index `i`, let `E` count leaves free relative to the extraction site. Sharing two occurrences changes size by

```text
Δq = E + 4 − i.
```

Inspection of `Q0` gives all possible repeated arguments and their minimum costs:

| Shared argument | Minimum Δq |
|---|---:|
| p | +7 |
| c | +7 |
| f | +10 |
| Application-branch e | +8 |

There are **no repeated compound arguments**, even allowing index shifts across binders. A single-occurrence extraction costs at least six bits: same-depth extraction adds at least six; variable extraction adds `E+5`; the only compound extraction crossing a binder is `x a` across `λy`, also costing six. An unused argument likewise cannot improve size.

I independently checked all **121 non-erasing inverse-β constructions** using the checker’s AST operations, asserted that each contracts back to `Q0`, and checked both laws for the minimizing witnesses. The table agrees with the calculation. No files were written.

**Where a successful term could still exist:** tag-specific simplification remains the most plausible opening. A source could exploit `b=K/F` without reducing to `Q0` for rigid `b`, or use interacting redexes whose combined compression is unavailable to one extraction. This exclusion does **not** establish incompressibility under multiple β-steps or optimality among arbitrary sources. It does rule out every single-let placement or single-subterm extraction from `Q0`, including placements beyond those already priced.

